  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThis solution improves upon the parent construction by using a multi-scale optimization approach that progressively refines the function across different resolutions. The core idea is to first find optimal placements of active intervals (where h=1) at coarse resolutions, then interpolate these onto fine grids, and use local search on these fine grids to minimize the maximum correlation. This leverages the Nystr\u00f6m extension and local refinement to escape local optima. The approach explicitly handles the many-integer-shift evaluation problem by focusing on the worst-case shift (closest to the actual continuous shift) and using active length instead of explicit function values for efficiency. Key bottlenecks addressed are the insensitivity of the parent solution to coarse-scale structure and the high dimensionality of higher-resolution cases.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Input: h_length (how much of current solution to use), current_best_array (current best solution at original resolution)\n- Represents internal state at request.time.next_check\n- Boundaries: 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i, integral constraint: sum(h) = h_length/2 exactly (after projection)\n- Optimize: minimize C5 = max_{shift=(-h_length+1 to h_length-1)} corr(shift) * dx, where dx=2/n, corr(shift) is the (weighted) overlap correlation\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Input resolution calculation:\n    best_n = 4*(current_iteration)^0.5\n\n2. Current_best_leech:\n    previous_solution_in_higher_res = interpolate(current_best_array) to target_length\n    current_best_leech = learn_overlap_patterns_from(previous_solution)\n\n3. Hierarchical descent:\n    Phase1: Global optimization in L2 space:\n    - Build candidate_pool of (position,length) tuples for active segments\n    - Ascend through resolutions while enforcing active length proportion\n    - Apply pattern discovery: worst_shift analysis\n\n4. Projection to feasible set:\n    Use linear projection to enforce integral and [0,1] bounds\n    Handling: min(h,1) + (0 where needed), ensure integral by proportional adjustment\n\n5. Smart gradient estimation:\n    - Use first-order correlation to backpropagate through transition layers\n    - Focus gradient on the worst shifts for faster convergence\n\n6. Resolution scheduling:\n    - Smoothness: \n        coarse \u2192 medium \u2192 high\n    - Overlap reduction: step(1) to step(0.5) in shift space\n\nSearch choices:\n- DNA: active segments: fixed or ...\n- Gradual learning from high-resolution parents\n- Balance between exploration and exploitation\n\nBudget and robustness:\n- Primary time allocation: 50% adaptation, 30% descent, 20% pollution\n- Death threshold: rising approximation error\n\nValidation:\n- maintain_derivative_consistency()\n- always project Feasible after each descent step\n\nreturn last_known_best_overlap",
